WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.395 --> 00:00:08.999
Four-year-old Alexandre Dumas carried his father's gun up the stairs to the attic.

2
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.463
He had been told that God had taken his father to heaven, so the boy was determined to

3
00:00:14.523 --> 00:00:16.685
kill God in revenge for this injustice.

4
00:00:17.766 --> 00:00:23.651
The youngster's boldness signaled the man he would become—a man of action, who went to war and fought

5
00:00:23.711 --> 00:00:25.072
duels to defend his honor.

6
00:00:26.113 --> 00:00:29.856
He was also a man who loved life and was always surrounded by friends.

7
00:00:31.008 --> 00:00:37.791
He was a master storyteller—able to weave adventure into stories that still delight audiences and readers around the

8
00:00:37.831 --> 00:00:46.855
world. Alexandre Dumas was born on July 24, 1802, in the village of Villers-Cotterêts, near Paris, France.

9
00:00:47.895 --> 00:00:53.117
His father, the son of a marquis and of a black slave, was a general in Napoleon's army.

10
00:00:54.078 --> 00:01:00.524
When Napoleon refused to pay the general's pension because Alexandre's father had criticized him, the family became very

11
00:01:00.584 --> 00:01:07.646
poor. At the general's death, the poverty worsened, and Alexandre's mother could not afford schooling for her son.

12
00:01:08.687 --> 00:01:14.548
But Alexandre experienced a youth rich in adventure—playing, hunting, and fishing with other young scamps.

13
00:01:15.789 --> 00:01:20.370
When he finally went to school at age ten, he quickly earned the respect of his peers by

14
00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:21.110
fighting a bully.

15
00:01:21.611 --> 00:01:24.311
He learned little but developed beautiful penmanship.

16
00:01:25.512 --> 00:01:30.234
As Alexandre grew up, he always tried to live up to the stories of his father, a great

17
00:01:30.314 --> 00:01:36.538
warrior who single-handedly defended a bridge against Austrian troops and a man who had the nerve to stand

18
00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:37.598
up to Napoleon.

19
00:01:38.919 --> 00:01:44.382
In 1823, Dumas moved to Paris, the center of literature and culture in France.

20
00:01:45.483 --> 00:01:51.406
He used his excellent penmanship to obtain a job copying documents for the Duke of Orléans, and in

21
00:01:51.446 --> 00:01:54.588
his spare time, he wrote plays and read voraciously.

22
00:01:55.552 --> 00:02:00.493
Confident of his talent as a writer, he said, "I will live by my penmanship now, but the

23
00:02:00.513 --> 00:02:03.014
time will come when I shall live by my pen."

24
00:02:04.415 --> 00:02:10.536
He wrote several plays with a friend—two comedies were presented in Paris theaters—but the direction of his writing

25
00:02:10.616 --> 00:02:12.837
changed when he saw Shakespeare's Hamlet.

26
00:02:14.097 --> 00:02:19.939
He liked the English playwright's use of history and said he felt like "someone blind from birth whose

27
00:02:19.999 --> 00:02:23.380
sight is suddenly restored and who discovers a whole new world."

28
00:02:24.748 --> 00:02:30.732
The inspiration for his next play, Henry III and His Court, came from a real event: a nobleman,

29
00:02:31.013 --> 00:02:36.656
finding his wife unfaithful to him, gave her the choice to either kill herself or arrange a meeting

30
00:02:36.696 --> 00:02:38.458
with her lover so he can be punished.

31
00:02:39.779 --> 00:02:46.323
Dumas developed this incident into a play about the politics of France in the 1500s—full of snappy dialogue,

32
00:02:46.623 --> 00:02:48.665
daring heroes, and hateful villains.

33
00:02:49.485 --> 00:02:52.768
Produced in 1829, the show was a huge success.

34
00:02:54.156 --> 00:03:00.038
Dumas became known for his ability to make historical events and characters more exciting and dramatic using his

35
00:03:00.158 --> 00:03:02.398
own experiences and vivid imagination.

36
00:03:03.779 --> 00:03:11.981
During the 1830s, newspapers in France printed stories by chapters in successive issues—known as serial novels—to attract readers.

37
00:03:13.182 --> 00:03:21.184
Between 1844 and 1846, Dumas wrote The Three Musketeers and The Count of Monte Cristo in serial form.

38
00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:28.182
Packed with the adventures and rapid-fire dialogue of his plays, these two novels are his most famous works.

39
00:03:29.322 --> 00:03:36.384
The Three Musketeers gave the world the heroes D'Artagnan, Athos, Porthos, and Aramis, as well as the quote

40
00:03:36.845 --> 00:03:38.685
"One for all and all for one."

41
00:03:39.705 --> 00:03:45.687
Pitting Dumas' heroes against the evil Cardinal Richelieu in defense of the queen of France, the book brought

42
00:03:45.747 --> 00:03:47.508
seventeenth-century France to life.

43
00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:54.631
In The Count of Monte Cristo, the hero, Edmond Dantès, is falsely accused by a man who wants

44
00:03:54.672 --> 00:03:57.613
to marry Dantès' fiancée and is sent to prison.

45
00:03:58.794 --> 00:04:02.136
There, he learns of a treasure on the island of Monte Cristo.

46
00:04:03.016 --> 00:04:09.260
After a daring escape, he uses the treasure to reward his benefactors and punish those responsible for his

47
00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:18.320
misfortunes. The theme in both books—righting wrongs and obtaining revenge against abuses by the powerful—echoes the writer's feelings

48
00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.721
toward the abuses his father suffered at the hands of Napoleon.

49
00:04:23.042 --> 00:04:24.622
Dumas was a prolific writer.

50
00:04:25.142 --> 00:04:30.704
During the 1830s, at least one of his plays was produced each year; in some years, four or

51
00:04:30.784 --> 00:04:36.385
five. In one year, 1844, he completed four novels and began a fifth.

52
00:04:37.546 --> 00:04:42.267
Over his lifetime, he wrote about three hundred literary works, including a cookbook.

53
00:04:43.387 --> 00:04:49.018
He worked ten to fourteen hours a day, but was criticized for using other writers to conduct research,

54
00:04:49.318 --> 00:04:52.139
develop outlines, and sometimes create first drafts.

55
00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:54.521
This infuriated Dumas.

56
00:04:55.181 --> 00:04:58.963
He even dueled with a collaborator who accused him of stealing his work.

57
00:04:59.964 --> 00:05:05.186
Dumas' supporters point out that he was able to write a novel or a play in days without

58
00:05:05.266 --> 00:05:09.289
help and that none of his collaborators ever wrote a successful work alone.

59
00:05:10.409 --> 00:05:17.857
It took Dumas' genius—his special ability to combine action, dialogue, and suspense—to turn history into exciting novels.

60
00:05:19.278 --> 00:05:24.941
Dumas had to work hard because he needed money for his frequent trips abroad, involvement in politics, and

61
00:05:25.041 --> 00:05:25.882
rich social life.

62
00:05:26.942 --> 00:05:33.045
Dumas lived outside France for many years; his journeys became the source for popular travel books, in which

63
00:05:33.105 --> 00:05:36.407
he wove anecdotes with facts about the various regions he visited.

64
00:05:37.528 --> 00:05:43.331
Throughout his life, he participated in political activities to help the cause of democracy and to try to

65
00:05:43.371 --> 00:05:44.712
live up to his father's memory.

66
00:05:45.824 --> 00:05:52.630
For instance, in 1830, he joined the fight against France's king, Charles X, and seized more than a

67
00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:56.092
ton of explosives for the uprising.

68
00:05:56.192 --> 00:06:02.998
In 1860, with his schooner, the Emma, he transported guns for the Italian fight for independence and sailed

69
00:06:03.038 --> 00:06:05.040
from port to port, inciting revolt.

70
00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:08.262
But above all, he lived for those he loved.

71
00:06:09.523 --> 00:06:14.067
He had dozens of mistresses and acknowledged three children, each by a different mother.

72
00:06:14.947 --> 00:06:18.893
His son, also named Alexandre Dumas, became a famous playwright.

73
00:06:18.913 --> 00:06:26.495
(To avoid confusion, the elder Alexandre is referred to as Dumas pere, meaning "father," and the younger, as

74
00:06:26.675 --> 00:06:28.496
Dumas fils, meaning "son.")

75
00:06:29.556 --> 00:06:34.697
He supported his mistresses and children and always kept a champagne glass full of money ready to aid

76
00:06:34.777 --> 00:06:35.558
friends in need.

77
00:06:36.558 --> 00:06:42.479
And he had many friends; he invited six hundred people to celebrate the completion of his extravagant home,

78
00:06:42.779 --> 00:06:44.320
the Château of Monte Cristo!

79
00:06:45.492 --> 00:06:52.278
Unfortunately, even Dumas' substantial income could not keep up with his lavish lifestyle, and he had to sell

80
00:06:52.358 --> 00:06:54.560
Monte Cristo at a fraction of its cost.

81
00:06:55.681 --> 00:07:00.024
As the end of his life approached, he was unable to write enough to pay his bills and

82
00:07:00.044 --> 00:07:00.905
became destitute.

83
00:07:01.826 --> 00:07:07.971
Dumas' adventures ended on December 5, 1870, when he died of a stroke at his son's home in

84
00:07:08.091 --> 00:07:09.012
Puys, France.

85
00:07:10.373 --> 00:07:18.450
Alexandre Dumas pere experienced love, war, and intrigue through his travels and friendships with both commoners and royalty.

86
00:07:19.511 --> 00:07:24.914
He made many fortunes and lost them all in pursuit of a meaningful, adventurous life.

87
00:07:25.955 --> 00:07:30.677
He achieved his goal of becoming worthy of his father's memory, but he wondered whether he would be

88
00:07:30.737 --> 00:07:31.838
remembered as a writer.

89
00:07:32.859 --> 00:07:39.482
The answer is clear: over 150 years after his death, his work lives on not only in books

90
00:07:39.863 --> 00:07:44.191
but also in films, cartoons, plays, and television programs.

91
00:07:45.493 --> 00:07:52.022
His genius continues to inform audiences and make them laugh and cry, just as his life inspires people

92
00:07:52.122 --> 00:07:53.824
to live their own lives to the fullest.
